Package qualification
=====================

Copyright 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

Use qore-packagers/qore-testing for development snapshots. Qualifying a snapshot
does not publish a final release. Repeat acceptance on the exact frozen release
artifacts before promoting them. Keep each run's source manifest, image IDs,
build dependency inventory, .buildinfo, .changes, logs and package checksums.
Do not put results or generated source archives inside the checkout.

The helpers run local rootless Podman containers and retain logs outside them;
they never install packages on the development host. Pull the target base image
first and pass its digest when repeatability is required. Distribution and
architecture qualification are separate: an Ubuntu build is not a Debian build.

Clean builds
------------

Prepare the source with tools/prepare-debian-source.py as described in
README.source. Verify source signatures/provenance before building downloaded
sources. For Ubuntu 26.04, provide the testing PPA's public archive key and a
deb822 .sources file using Signed-By: /usr/share/keyrings/qore-testing.gpg:

  export QORE_TEST_APT_KEY=/path/to/verified-qore-testing.gpg
  export QORE_TEST_APT_SOURCE=/path/to/qore-testing.sources
  tools/build-debian-package.sh /path/to/qore_source.dsc \
    /path/to/new-build-results docker.io/library/ubuntu:26.04

The current testing archive fingerprint is
064EECD97DD60E822006EC8A2324C51F64E7B4EA; verify it against Launchpad before
trusting a downloaded key. This is the archive key, not an uploader's key.
APT provisioning has network access. Compilation, documentation and tests run
without networking as an unprivileged user with HOME=/sbuild-nonexistent.
All Build-Depends must resolve; the helper rejects nocheck. Binary Lintian
errors fail the run. Inspect warnings too. The provisioned image is retained
and identified in build-image.txt for diagnosis.

The default options match the testing PPA: parallel=8 noautodbgsym. For debug
package qualification set QORE_DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=parallel=8. For nodoc builds
set QORE_DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=nodoc and QORE_DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS='parallel=8 nodoc'.
Record and compare matching profiles; a nodoc build cannot qualify qore-doc.
The helper performs full binary builds; use sbuild -B separately to qualify
the architecture-only build path.

Static artifact checks and reproducibility
-----------------------------------------

  tools/check-debian-artifacts.py inspect /path/to/build-results \
    --output /path/to/inspection.json

This checks every shipped runtime ELF for position independence, full RELRO,
non-executable stack, absence of RPATH/RUNPATH and text relocations, and validates
the versioned AOT metadata chain. A binary .changes file, when present, must
match every referenced artifact's size and SHA-256 digest, and every inspected
.deb/.ddeb must be listed. This is integrity verification, not signature verification.
Without .changes, the report explicitly records that no manifest was supplied.
Detached debug packages are inspected without applying runtime ELF hardening
requirements to their non-loadable files. To qualify a debug-enabled build, run:

  tools/check-debian-artifacts.py inspect /path/to/build-results \
    --require-debug-symbols --output /path/to/debug-inspection.json

Every runtime ELF must have a matching detached debug file with DWARF, the same
build ID, the standard /usr/lib/debug/.build-id location, and a filename/CRC
matching its .gnu_debuglink. Both Ubuntu .ddeb and
Debian -dbgsym .deb packages are supported. Missing symbols fail this check.
Keep each version/profile in its own directory. Compiler fortification and
stack-protector coverage still require build-flag review; ELF checks alone do
not establish those properties for every function.

Build the same source twice in fresh containers, with the same dependency
versions and build options. Vary the build path/time/locale deliberately when
investigating reproducibility. Inspect each build, then compare:

  tools/check-debian-artifacts.py compare /path/to/first.json /path/to/second.json \
    --output /path/to/comparison.json

Any differing package bytes make the comparison fail. It also lists changed
payload/control members, including modes, timestamps, ownership, symlink targets
and content hashes. Matching file inventories or successful tests alone are not
reproducibility. Use diffoscope on differing packages for deeper analysis.

Installation, upgrades and removal
---------------------------------

  tools/qualify-debian-packages.sh /path/to/qore_source.dsc \
    /path/to/packages /path/to/new-install-results docker.io/library/ubuntu:26.04 \
    /path/to/previous-packages

The same optional signed APT settings above supply dependency backports. The
helper checks the distribution/version, installs real older artifacts before
upgrading, runs the installed runtime tests after upgrade, and validates a
minimal installation without Recommends before installing test dependencies.
It runs all four autopkgtests, then uses dpkg to coinstall the exact supplied
artifacts with dependencies checked and version downgrades refused. This also
handles rebuilt artifacts whose version already exists in the testing archive.
It verifies files with dpkg -V, purges, and reinstalls the runtime. The autopkgtest null
backend is confined to the disposable container; never run it on the host.

Omit the previous-package directory for a first-version installation test; the
report then explicitly records that upgrades were not tested. A PASS marker
does not erase that limitation. Test every actual supported upgrade baseline.
There are currently no previously distributed standalone module packages to
migrate. JSON, YAML and all other bundled modules remain in qore-stdlib.
Source-built installations under /usr/local or /opt are not dpkg upgrades:
test their chosen migration separately without deleting unmanaged files.

Independent modules
-------------------

Build independent module packages against the installed libqore-dev and qore,
using native builds for the target distribution. Keep modules shipped in Qore's
modules/ and qlib/ trees in Qore's packages. Do not create duplicate JSON/YAML
packages. Before packaging a separate module, inventory its native libraries,
Qore dependencies, vendored sources/licenses, user modules/resources, tests,
documentation and generated metadata. Preserve AOT trailers when stripping
user modules, just as Qore does. Run tests against installed artifacts.

The initial external-module SDK check builds UUID from a clean committed source
archive, using only installed Qore headers/tools/libraries, and runs its tests.
This verifies that SDK path but does not qualify every module or a module .deb.
External module repositories require their own packaging commits and checks;
database/service modules also need their integration services. Proprietary SDKs,
large vendored runtimes, JNI JAR inventories and architecture restrictions must
be resolved per module rather than hidden by a generic package template.

Release acceptance
------------------

Before general publication, freeze the version and source, repeat builds and
installed-artifact checks, assess ABI compatibility with any distributed
libqore20 snapshots, qualify debug symbols, and resolve reproducibility findings.
Test the actual Qorus image and independent modules against that release.
Retain license/provenance inventories and assign dependency/security maintenance
ownership. Support only distribution/architecture combinations with successful
evidence. Sign the reviewed source uploads with debsign and upload only source
.changes files to the testing PPA as documented in backports/README. Promotion
to a release archive is a later, explicit release action.
